{"title":"2001 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To promote her school's annual Kite Olympics, Genevieve makes a small kite and a large kite for a bulletin board display. The kites look like the one in the diagram. For her small kite Genevieve draws the kite on a one-inch grid. For the large kite she triples both the height and width of the entire grid.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/f/f1f9469db4540c2a66686ef837e33ea3746e6e8a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] for (int a = 0; a &lt; 7; ++a) { for (int b = 0; b &lt; 8; ++b) { dot((a,b)); } }  draw((3,0)--(0,5)--(3,7)--(6,5)--cycle); [/asy]\" width=\"218\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>The large kite is covered with gold foil. The foil is cut from a rectangular piece that just covers the entire grid. How many square inches of waste material are cut off from the four corners?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/1/e4138553d31a712ef483b4fb09d96b0f70003c97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 63 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 72 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 180 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 189 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 264$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"424\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The large grid has dimensions three times that of the small grid, so its dimensions are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/e/75ef16526b470f70d0881bf39cb67ddc2bb1d6e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(6)\\times3(7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/7/307ac8ef268539c17dd449b55f3238019880c0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18\\times21$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" />, so the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9eec1d45d5721b064f5ec7e8a177f50aa3ad24d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(18)(21)=378$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />. The area of the kite is half of the area of the rectangle as you can see, so the area of the waste material is also half the area of the rectangle.  Thus, the area of the waste material is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/8/778fa20131e33705528a9f3d2fddccce4dd1a4db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$378/2=\\boxed{189\\textbf{ (D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"146\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8\" title=\"2001 AMC 8\">2001 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"2001 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AMC_8_Answer_Key\" title=\"2001 AMC 8 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.ph